<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Hohenzollern</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hohenzollerns, Hohenzollern dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Hohenzollern is a German dynasty named after its castle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hechingen">Hechingen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>. Its Franconian line, descended from Frederick of Zollern, burgrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> from 1192, gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1415 and later ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and, from 1871 to 1918, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>. A Swabian line kept the principalities of Hohenzollern, and from its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a> branch came the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern.jpg" alt="House of Hohenzollern" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Намѣсникъ · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wappen_Hohenzollern_2.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The counts sat with the Swabian counts in the Imperial Diet, belonged to the Swabian Imperial Circle and increasingly used the name <b>Hohenzollern</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The first <b>Hohenzollern</b>, Frederick I, made his authority real by breaking the castles of robber barons such as the Quitzow family in 1414 with heavy artillery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The first family, the Raabs, died out around 1190, and Frederick of Zollern, a Swabian noble married to the Raabs' heiress, became burgrave in 1192, founding the <b>Franconian Hohenzollern</b> line.</blockquote><a href="/state/burgraviate-of-nuremberg">Burgraviate of Nuremberg</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Jülich succession crisis, the Treaty of Xanten in 1614 gave Cleves to Brandenburg, giving the <b>Hohenzollerns</b> their first foothold in the west.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-cleves">Duchy of Cleves</a></li><li><blockquote>His <b>Hohenzollern</b> heirs held it until 1621, when Johann Georg lost it for supporting the Bohemian revolt.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-krnov">Duchy of Krnov</a></li><li><blockquote>Its merchants gained privileges from the Salian and Hohenstaufen emperors, and in 1219 Frederick II's Great Charter of Freedom laid the basis for its self-government, gradually freeing it from the <b>Hohenzollern</b> burgraves who held the castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-nuremberg">Free Imperial City of Nuremberg</a></li></ul></section>
